1
00:00:00,534 --> 00:00:05,474
Every Monday morning, at exactly six
a.m., Daniel Hale got an email from

2
00:00:05,494 --> 00:00:07,201
someone he'd never met.

3
00:00:07,481 --> 00:00:12,421
It was always one sentence. What
the stock market would do that week.

4
00:00:12,991 --> 00:00:16,571
Up. Down. Down. Up.

5
00:00:16,771 --> 00:00:20,261
The sender was never wrong. Not once.

6
00:00:20,281 --> 00:00:22,241
Ten weeks in a row.

7
00:00:22,491 --> 00:00:26,561
The odds of guessing that are one
in one thousand and twenty-four.

8
00:00:26,771 --> 00:00:32,162
On the eleventh Monday, the email
asked for fifty thousand dollars.

9
00:00:32,442 --> 00:00:36,822
Daniel had a wife, a mortgage, and
a daughter about to start college.

10
00:00:36,842 --> 00:00:40,312
And ten perfect predictions
sitting in his inbox.

11
00:00:40,672 --> 00:00:42,982
Nobody knows who sent those emails.

12
00:00:43,152 --> 00:00:46,713
But by the end of this, you'll
know exactly how it worked.

13
00:00:52,633 --> 00:00:55,743
The first one arrived
on a Monday in March.

14
00:00:56,093 --> 00:01:02,633
No logo. No link. No name. Just a
subject line that said: Week one.

15
00:01:02,653 --> 00:01:04,380
And a single sentence.

16
00:01:04,670 --> 00:01:06,930
The market will go down this week.

17
00:01:07,250 --> 00:01:11,150
Daniel read it twice. He
always reads things twice.

18
00:01:11,170 --> 00:01:12,561
Then he deleted it.

19
00:01:12,831 --> 00:01:15,731
That Friday, the market closed down.

20
00:01:16,051 --> 00:01:18,498
He didn't notice. Why would he?

21
00:01:18,518 --> 00:01:21,121
It was a coin flip, and the coin landed.

22
00:01:21,381 --> 00:01:24,961
The next Monday, at six
a.m., the phone lit up again.

23
00:01:24,981 --> 00:01:27,761
Week two. The market will go up.

24
00:01:28,041 --> 00:01:29,391
It went up.

25
00:01:29,641 --> 00:01:33,281
Week three. Down. It went down.

26
00:01:33,641 --> 00:01:37,671
By week four, Daniel had
stopped deleting them.

27
00:01:39,601 --> 00:01:43,861
To understand what happened next,
you have to understand Daniel.

28
00:01:44,201 --> 00:01:48,181
Forty-four years old. He scheduled
trucks for a freight company outside

29
00:01:48,201 --> 00:01:51,602
Columbus. Same desk for twenty-two years.

30
00:01:51,802 --> 00:01:54,892
His wife, Maya, worked nights as a nurse.

31
00:01:54,912 --> 00:01:58,622
They traded off the car, the
dishes, and the worrying.

32
00:01:58,822 --> 00:02:02,962
And their daughter, Lily, had just
been accepted to an engineering program

33
00:02:02,982 --> 00:02:04,762
four hundred miles away.

34
00:02:05,002 --> 00:02:08,992
The tuition letter came the
same week as email number four.

35
00:02:09,272 --> 00:02:11,892
Daniel had a spreadsheet for everything.

36
00:02:11,912 --> 00:02:16,292
The mortgage. The car.
And one tab called Lily.

37
00:02:16,312 --> 00:02:20,692
Fifty-two thousand dollars, saved
one paycheck at a time, since the day

38
00:02:20,712 --> 00:02:21,872
she was born.

39
00:02:22,262 --> 00:02:25,442
So he did what anyone with
a spreadsheet would do.

40
00:02:25,462 --> 00:02:27,702
He started tracking the emails.

41
00:02:27,952 --> 00:02:32,682
Week four said up. It
went up. Four for four.

42
00:02:32,882 --> 00:02:35,862
On week five, the email said down.

43
00:02:35,912 --> 00:02:40,152
And for the first time in his life,
Daniel bet against the market.

44
00:02:40,592 --> 00:02:44,519
Five hundred dollars. If
the market fell, he'd win.

45
00:02:44,539 --> 00:02:47,025
If it rose, he'd lose all of it.

46
00:02:47,225 --> 00:02:54,695
On Friday, the market fell. Daniel made
eleven hundred dollars before lunch.

47
00:02:55,095 --> 00:03:00,485
Five for five. And that's when
the emails started using his name.

48
00:03:02,515 --> 00:03:05,375
Week six. Good morning, Daniel.

49
00:03:05,395 --> 00:03:06,145
Up.

50
00:03:06,478 --> 00:03:09,125
He bet two thousand dollars. It went up.

51
00:03:09,405 --> 00:03:12,345
Week seven. Down. He bet four thousand.

52
00:03:12,365 --> 00:03:13,505
It went down.

53
00:03:13,785 --> 00:03:16,615
He told his best friend,
Marcus, over a beer.

54
00:03:17,145 --> 00:03:20,725
Marcus laughed. Then Marcus
asked to see the emails.

55
00:03:20,745 --> 00:03:22,825
Then Marcus stopped laughing.

56
00:03:23,135 --> 00:03:25,435
Here's what was going on in Daniel's head.

57
00:03:25,455 --> 00:03:28,795
And honestly, it's what
would be going on in yours.

58
00:03:29,075 --> 00:03:32,863
One right guess is luck.
Two is a coincidence.

59
00:03:32,883 --> 00:03:36,396
But every week, the streak
got harder to explain.

60
00:03:36,746 --> 00:03:39,926
Five in a row: one in thirty-two.

61
00:03:39,946 --> 00:03:42,806
Seven: one in a hundred and twenty-eight.

62
00:03:42,826 --> 00:03:46,158
Eight: one in two hundred and fifty-six.

63
00:03:46,438 --> 00:03:50,708
Week eight said up. Daniel
bet ten thousand dollars.

64
00:03:50,891 --> 00:03:53,548
The most he had ever risked on anything.

65
00:03:54,488 --> 00:03:57,174
It went up. Eight for eight.

66
00:03:57,812 --> 00:04:00,234
Week nine almost broke him.

67
00:04:02,194 --> 00:04:04,034
Week nine said up.

68
00:04:04,244 --> 00:04:09,152
On Monday, the market dropped.
Tuesday, it dropped again.

69
00:04:09,172 --> 00:04:12,594
By Thursday, it had its
worst day in months.

70
00:04:12,854 --> 00:04:16,802
Daniel stopped sleeping. He refreshed
the chart in the bathroom at work.

71
00:04:16,822 --> 00:04:20,014
He had twelve thousand
dollars riding on this one.

72
00:04:20,614 --> 00:04:24,274
Friday. Three p.m. One hour left.

73
00:04:24,294 --> 00:04:25,634
Still down.

74
00:04:25,884 --> 00:04:29,624
And then, in the last forty
minutes, the market turned.

75
00:04:29,644 --> 00:04:34,454
A speech, a rumor, a rate.
Nobody could really explain it.

76
00:04:34,654 --> 00:04:37,504
At four o'clock, the market closed up.

77
00:04:37,914 --> 00:04:40,094
By less than half a percent.

78
00:04:40,494 --> 00:04:42,464
Nine for nine.

79
00:04:42,744 --> 00:04:45,444
That night, Daniel didn't celebrate.

80
00:04:45,464 --> 00:04:48,890
He sat in his car in the
driveway, and he said it out loud.

81
00:04:49,512 --> 00:04:50,584
Who are you?

82
00:04:52,544 --> 00:04:56,824
Week ten. Down. Of course it went down.

83
00:04:57,094 --> 00:05:02,194
Ten for ten. One in one
thousand and twenty-four.

84
00:05:02,394 --> 00:05:07,304
And on the eleventh Monday, at
six a.m., there was no prediction.

85
00:05:07,744 --> 00:05:09,554
There was an offer.

86
00:05:09,844 --> 00:05:13,851
You've seen what I can do.
Fifty-two more weeks of predictions.

87
00:05:13,871 --> 00:05:16,272
The fee is fifty thousand dollars.

88
00:05:16,292 --> 00:05:17,784
You have until Friday.

89
00:05:18,304 --> 00:05:23,014
Fifty thousand. Almost exactly
the number on the tab called Lily.

90
00:05:23,585 --> 00:05:27,354
Daniel ran the numbers. If the predictions
kept coming, fifty thousand could

91
00:05:27,374 --> 00:05:30,044
become half a million
by the end of the year.

92
00:05:30,401 --> 00:05:32,594
College, ten times over.

93
00:05:33,034 --> 00:05:36,024
Maya found the spreadsheet
on Thursday night.

94
00:05:36,294 --> 00:05:40,604
She asked him one question.
How do you know he's real?

95
00:05:40,854 --> 00:05:44,204
And Daniel said the
only thing he could say.

96
00:05:45,307 --> 00:05:47,534
Because he's never been wrong.

97
00:05:47,764 --> 00:05:51,034
On Friday morning, he wired the money.

98
00:05:51,267 --> 00:05:54,687
But Daniel never asked the
one question that mattered.

99
00:05:54,707 --> 00:06:00,414
Not, how was he right? But, how
many people did he start with?

100
00:06:02,374 --> 00:06:05,394
Let's go back to the very first Monday.

101
00:06:05,704 --> 00:06:09,124
That morning, the email
didn't go to just Daniel.

102
00:06:09,144 --> 00:06:12,864
It went to ten thousand, two
hundred and forty people.

103
00:06:13,134 --> 00:06:15,674
Half of them were told
the market would go up.

104
00:06:15,694 --> 00:06:18,114
The other half were told it would go down.

105
00:06:18,314 --> 00:06:21,164
On Friday, one group was right.

106
00:06:21,184 --> 00:06:24,113
The other group never heard
from the prophet again.

107
00:06:24,393 --> 00:06:28,613
After week one, five thousand, one
hundred and twenty people were left.

108
00:06:28,633 --> 00:06:31,703
Split them in half again. Up. Down.

109
00:06:31,943 --> 00:06:35,603
After week two: two thousand,
five hundred and sixty.

110
00:06:35,623 --> 00:06:38,003
Week three: twelve hundred and eighty.

111
00:06:38,023 --> 00:06:40,553
Week four: six hundred and forty.

112
00:06:40,993 --> 00:06:44,653
Every single week, half
the believers vanished.

113
00:06:44,673 --> 00:06:48,133
And the ones who were
left saw a perfect record.

114
00:06:48,333 --> 00:06:53,513
After week eight: forty.
Week nine: twenty.

115
00:06:54,195 --> 00:06:55,383
Week ten…

116
00:06:55,663 --> 00:07:00,483
Ten people. Ten people who had watched
someone predict the market ten times

117
00:07:00,503 --> 00:07:02,903
in a row. Perfectly.

118
00:07:03,263 --> 00:07:06,336
Daniel wasn't chosen. He survived.

119
00:07:06,356 --> 00:07:10,603
Like a coin that lands heads ten times
in a row, because ten thousand coins

120
00:07:10,623 --> 00:07:11,709
were flipped.

121
00:07:11,996 --> 00:07:14,559
The prophet didn't know
anything about the market.

122
00:07:14,579 --> 00:07:19,679
Not one thing. Every prediction was a
guess, split so that someone was always

123
00:07:19,699 --> 00:07:20,599
right.

124
00:07:20,879 --> 00:07:23,739
This trick is real,
and it even has a name.

125
00:07:23,759 --> 00:07:27,779
In Jordan Ellenberg's book, How Not
to Be Wrong, it's the parable of the

126
00:07:27,799 --> 00:07:32,139
Baltimore stockbroker. Ten thousand,
two hundred and forty letters.

127
00:07:32,159 --> 00:07:34,211
Ten perfect records.

128
00:07:34,548 --> 00:07:38,301
In two thousand eight, the magician
Derren Brown did a version of it on

129
00:07:38,321 --> 00:07:40,781
British television, with horse races.

130
00:07:40,801 --> 00:07:43,981
Seven thousand, seven hundred
and seventy-six people.

131
00:07:44,001 --> 00:07:48,651
Five races. One woman left,
convinced she'd found a system.

132
00:07:48,911 --> 00:07:52,651
And it costs almost
nothing. An email is free.

133
00:07:52,671 --> 00:07:57,251
Out of ten perfect records, the
prophet only needs a few to say yes.

134
00:07:57,681 --> 00:08:03,101
Seven of the ten paid. Three hundred
and fifty thousand dollars, for ten

135
00:08:03,121 --> 00:08:04,851
weeks of coin flips.

136
00:08:06,751 --> 00:08:11,391
Week twelve's email said up.
The market fell three percent.

137
00:08:11,741 --> 00:08:15,271
Week thirteen, there was no email at all.

138
00:08:15,521 --> 00:08:18,541
Daniel wrote back. The address bounced.

139
00:08:18,561 --> 00:08:21,841
The account was gone. So was the money.

140
00:08:22,071 --> 00:08:24,851
He told Maya in the kitchen, at six a.m.

141
00:08:24,871 --> 00:08:28,441
on a Monday. The same time
the emails used to come.

142
00:08:28,671 --> 00:08:33,211
Lily still went to college. Two years
at a community college first, working

143
00:08:33,231 --> 00:08:35,861
weekends. She's an engineer now.

144
00:08:35,924 --> 00:08:37,881
She doesn't talk about that spring.

145
00:08:38,141 --> 00:08:42,281
And Daniel? He still wakes
up every Monday at six.

146
00:08:42,301 --> 00:08:46,012
Not because he believes.
Because he can't stop.

147
00:08:46,292 --> 00:08:48,672
Here's the part that isn't fiction.

148
00:08:48,902 --> 00:08:53,282
In twenty twenty-four, Americans
reported losing five point seven billion

149
00:08:53,302 --> 00:08:57,402
dollars to investment scams. More than
any other kind of fraud, according

150
00:08:57,422 --> 00:08:59,256
to the Federal Trade Commission.

151
00:08:59,486 --> 00:09:02,266
Today, the prophet doesn't need email.

152
00:09:02,286 --> 00:09:05,146
He runs a trading group.
A signals channel.

153
00:09:05,166 --> 00:09:08,516
An account with screenshots
of a perfect record.

154
00:09:08,856 --> 00:09:13,316
So if a perfect record ever finds
you, don't ask how they were right.

155
00:09:13,336 --> 00:09:15,787
Ask how many people they started with.

156
00:09:16,147 --> 00:09:20,147
Because you never see the
people who got the other email.

157
00:09:22,069 --> 00:09:26,277
At the start, I told you nobody
knows who sent those emails.

158
00:09:26,917 --> 00:09:29,307
That isn't quite true.

159
00:09:29,857 --> 00:09:33,357
How do you think I know
Daniel reads everything twice?

160
00:09:33,377 --> 00:09:37,327
That he sat in his car in the
driveway, asking who I was?

161
00:09:37,361 --> 00:09:40,017
That exactly seven of the ten paid?

162
00:09:41,420 --> 00:09:44,617
Because I wrote them. Every single one.

163
00:09:44,637 --> 00:09:47,847
All ten thousand, two hundred and forty.

164
00:09:48,337 --> 00:09:51,807
Daniel was number four
thousand and ninety-one.

165
00:09:52,107 --> 00:09:56,243
And next Monday, at six
a.m., I start again.

166
00:09:57,013 --> 00:09:58,703
Check your inbox.
